<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vladimir II Monomakh</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1053–<a href="/years/1125">1125</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Vladimir Monomakh · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rurik-dynasty">Rurik dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vladimir Monomakh was Prince of Pereyaslavl and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chernigov">Chernigov</a> and, from 1113, Grand Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a>. A grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslav-the-wise">Yaroslav the Wise</a> and, through his mother, of the Byzantine emperor Constantine IX Monomachos, he led repeated campaigns against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Cumans</a> and took part in the princely congress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyubech">Lyubech</a> in 1097. His Instruction to his children is a classic of early Rus literature, and his sons ruled much of Rus after him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vladimir-ii-monomakh.jpg" alt="Vladimir II Monomakh" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Печать Владимира II Мономаха · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%9F%D0%B5%D1%87%D0%B0%D1%82%D1%8C_%D0%92%D0%BB%D0%B0%D0%B4%D0%B8%D0%BC%D0%B8%D1%80%D0%B0_II_%D0%9C%D0%BE%D0%BD%D0%BE%D0%BC%D0%B0%D1%85%D0%B0.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Vladimir was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vsevolod-i-of-kiev">Vsevolod Yaroslavich</a>, who reigned as Grand Prince Vsevolod I of Kiev from 1078 to 1093. In 1046 Vsevolod had made a diplomatic marriage to a relative of the Byzantine emperor Constantine IX Monomachos, from whom Vladimir probably took his nickname Monomakh. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primary-chronicle">Primary Chronicle</a> names his mother only as a Greek princess. A later story that he defeated Constantine and was given the name in a peace settlement is impossible, since the emperor died in 1055. The name first appears in a eulogy in the Kievan Chronicle and on his seal.</p><p>As a young prince Monomakh ruled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smolensk">Smolensk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chernigov">Chernigov</a> and led 13 campaigns in the name of his father. In 1068 he allied himself with the Cuman chief Bilge-Tegin. After 1094 his chief patrimony was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pereiaslav">Pereyaslav</a>, though he also controlled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostov">Rostov</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzdal">Suzdal</a> and other northern provinces, where he founded several towns, notably the one named Vladimir. His quarrel with his cousin Oleg began when Oleg made peace with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Polovtsians</a> to retake Chernigov, and it continued among their descendants.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Monomakh initiated three princely congresses, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyubech">Lyubech</a> in 1097, Vytachiv in 1100 and Dolobsk in 1103, to unite the princes against the steppe by allowing each princely branch to keep its patrimony. Between 1103 and 1111 the Rus princes raided the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Polovtsians</a> every year. In 1107 Monomakh defeated the Cuman khan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boniak">Boniak</a>, and in 1111, with Sviatopolk II, he led the army that defeated a Cuman force at the river Salnitsa, probably near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/izium">Izium</a>. These victories drew the Cumans into the steppes and prevented new raids. His Instruction states that he conducted 83 campaigns and made peace with the Polovtsians 19 times.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>When Sviatopolk II died in 1113, the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> revolted, attacking officials, wealthy citizens and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>. The ruling circles invited Monomakh, the most liked prince, to take the throne. He lowered the rate of interest on loans and issued decrees that prohibited usury, banned the enslavement of merchants indebted through accident, regulated the status of bondsmen and restricted the power of the boyars.</p><p>His statute was included in the revised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russkaya-pravda">Russkaya Pravda</a>. He wrote his Instruction to Children about 1117, a didactic and autobiographical work that condemns princely strife and promotes the idea of a unified state. He was buried in Saint Sophia Cathedral. Later generations remembered his reign as a golden age, before Rus was torn apart by internal struggles after the death of his son Mstislav in 1132.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vladimir II Monomakh” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From 1061 the Cumans, or Polovtsy, replaced them as the main steppe threat, routing the princes on the Alta River in 1068, until <b>Vladimir Monomakh</b> led joint campaigns deep into the steppe in 1103 and 1111.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus' · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The old centres were Rostov and Suzdal, and the town of Vladimir on the Klyazma was traditionally founded by <b>Vladimir Monomakh</b> around 1108.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vladimir-suzdal">Vladimir-Suzdal · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>He gave the land its law: Yaroslav the Wise (r. 1019–1054) wrote the first part of the Russkaya Pravda, and his sons and his grandson <b>Vladimir Monomakh</b> added to it.</blockquote><a href="/societies/kievan-rus">Social Hierarchy of Kievan Rus'</a></li><li><blockquote>Gleb Vseslavich made Minsk a power in its own right and fought Kiev and <b>Vladimir Monomakh</b> until he was captured and died in Kiev in 1119.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-minsk">Principality of Minsk</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Vladimir Monomakh</b> ruled here from 1094 to 1113 and led campaigns deep into the steppe.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-pereyaslavl">Principality of Pereyaslavl</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Vladimir Monomakh</b> gave them to his son Yuri, who around 1125 made Suzdal his seat, turning the region into an independent principality.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-rostov-suzdal">Principality of Rostov-Suzdal</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pereiaslav">Pereiaslav</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.07,31.45&amp;year=1099" aria-label="Pereiaslav on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5270 422 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Vladimir II Monomakh"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5720 692h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5720 692h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.07,31.45&amp;year=1099">What was here in 1099? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
